exec(open(__file__.rsplit("/",1)[0] + "/_h.py").read())
B("Numbers", """
1|God tells Moses to count the men of Israel who are twenty or older and can fight. The tribe of Levi is not counted, because it is set apart to care for the tent.
2|God tells Israel how to camp around the tent, three tribes on each side. Each tribe has its own flag.
3|The Levites are counted and given to God in place of the firstborn of Israel. Their families are given jobs caring for the tent and its furnishings.
4|God lists the work of the Levite families who carry the tent and its holy things when Israel travels.
5|God tells Israel to send unclean people outside the camp, to make things right when they wrong someone, and gives a test for a wife suspected of unfaithfulness.
6|God gives the Nazirite vow, in which a person dedicates himself or herself to God for a time. God also gives the priestly blessing: the Lord bless you and keep you.
7|The leaders of the twelve tribes bring gifts for the tent, one tribe each day. God speaks to Moses from above the ark.
8|The lamps are set up, and the Levites are cleansed and presented to God. They serve from age twenty-five and retire at fifty.
9|Israel keeps the Passover at Sinai. A cloud covers the tent by day and fire by night, and the people move only when it moves.
10|Silver trumpets are made to call the people. Israel leaves Sinai in order, with the ark leading. Moses asks his brother-in-law Hobab to guide them.
11|The people complain about eating only manna and want meat. God sends quail, and then a plague. He shares Moses' spirit with seventy elders.
12|Miriam and Aaron speak against Moses. God defends him, calling him the most humble man on earth. Miriam is struck with a skin disease and healed after seven days.
13|Moses sends twelve spies into Canaan. They bring back large fruit. Ten say the people are too strong, but Caleb and Joshua say Israel can take the land.
14|The people panic and want to go back to Egypt. God says that this generation will wander forty years in the desert and not enter the land, except Caleb and Joshua.
15|God gives more rules for offerings and for sins done by mistake. A man is stoned for gathering sticks on the Sabbath. Israel is told to wear tassels as a reminder of God's commands.
16|Korah, Dathan and Abiram rebel against Moses and Aaron. The earth opens and swallows them. The people complain and a plague follows.
17|God makes Aaron's staff bud and blossom to show that he has chosen Aaron's family for the priesthood.
18|God tells Aaron and the Levites their duties and what portion of the offerings and tithes belong to them. The Levites get no land, because the Lord is their inheritance.
19|God gives rules for the red cow, whose ashes are used in a water of cleansing for anyone who has touched a dead body.
20|Miriam dies. The people have no water, and Moses strikes the rock instead of speaking to it. God tells him he will not enter the land. Aaron's death follows soon after.
21|Israel is bitten by snakes. Moses raises a bronze snake on a pole, and everyone who looks at it lives. Israel defeats the kings Sihon and Og.
22|King Balak of Moab hires Balaam to curse Israel. Balaam's donkey sees an angel of the Lord and speaks to him.
23|Balaam builds altars with Balak, but God puts words in his mouth, and he blesses Israel instead of cursing.
24|Balaam blesses Israel again and says that a star will come out of Jacob and a scepter will rise from Israel.
25|Israel worships Baal of Peor and some men have relations with Moabite women. A plague strikes and ends when Phinehas acts to stop it.
26|After the plague a second census is taken. Only Caleb and Joshua remain from the first count.
27|The daughters of Zelophehad ask for their father's land, and God says daughters can inherit. God tells Moses to pick Joshua as his successor.
28|God lists the daily, weekly, monthly and yearly offerings Israel must bring.
29|God gives the offerings for the feasts of Trumpets, the Day of Atonement and Booths in the seventh month.
30|God gives rules about vows made by women, and when their fathers or husbands can cancel them.
31|Israel fights Midian and kills Balaam. The plunder is divided between the soldiers and the rest of the people, and part is given to the Lord.
32|The tribes of Reuben and Gad and half of Manasseh ask to settle east of the Jordan. Moses agrees if they first help the other tribes conquer the land.
33|This chapter lists the places Israel camped on the way from Egypt. God tells them to drive out the people in Canaan and destroy their idols.
34|God sets the boundaries of the land Israel will receive and names the men who will divide it among the tribes.
35|Israel is to give the Levites forty-eight towns to live in, including six cities of refuge, where someone who kills by accident can be safe.
36|The daughters of Zelophehad are told to marry within their tribe, so that the land does not pass to another tribe.
""")
B("Deuteronomy", """
1|Moses begins to speak to Israel across the Jordan, forty years after leaving Egypt. He reminds them how they left Sinai and refused to enter the land.
2|Moses reviews the long years of wandering. God tells Israel to pass by Edom, Moab and Ammon, and then they defeat King Sihon.
3|Israel defeats King Og of Bashan and takes his land east of the Jordan. Moses asks to enter the promised land, but God says no and tells him to prepare Joshua.
4|Moses urges Israel to keep God's laws, which will show other nations their wisdom. He warns them against making idols and says there is no other God.
5|Moses repeats the Ten Commandments. The people say they will do everything the Lord says.
6|Moses gives the greatest commandment: Hear, O Israel, the Lord our God, the Lord is one. Love him with all your heart, soul and strength. Teach it to your children.
7|God tells Israel to destroy the nations in the land and not intermarry with them. He loves Israel, not because they are many, but because he made a promise to their fathers.
8|Moses reminds Israel how God tested them in the desert and fed them with manna. People do not live by bread alone, but by every word from God.
9|Moses warns that Israel is not receiving the land because they are good, but because of God's promise. He recalls the golden calf and how he prayed for the people.
10|God writes new tablets and Moses puts them in the ark. Moses asks Israel to fear God, love him and care for the stranger, the orphan and the widow.
11|Moses urges Israel to love God and keep his commands, and says the land will be blessed if they do. He sets blessing and curse before them.
12|God commands Israel to worship at the one place he chooses, and to destroy the pagan altars. They may not eat blood.
13|Israel must not follow anyone who tries to lead them to other gods, even a prophet, a family member or a friend.
14|God lists clean and unclean animals, and tells Israel to give a tenth of their produce to him each year and share it with the poor and the Levites.
15|Every seventh year debts are cancelled and Hebrew slaves are set free. Israel is told to be open-handed toward the poor.
16|God gives rules for Passover, Weeks and Booths, and for appointing fair judges.
17|Moses sets rules about worship, courts and kings. The king must not gather many horses, wives or wealth, and must read God's law all his life.
18|God sets out what priests and Levites receive. Israel must not practice sorcery. God promises to raise up a prophet like Moses.
19|Israel is to set aside cities of refuge and keep landmarks in place. Two or three witnesses are needed for any charge.
20|God gives rules for war. Soldiers who are afraid or newly married may go home, and cities must be offered peace first.
21|God gives laws about unsolved murders, the rights of a firstborn, and rebellious sons. A person who is hanged must be buried before nightfall.
22|God gives many everyday laws: return a neighbor's lost animal, put a railing on the roof, and keep marriages and families safe.
23|God sets who may join the assembly of Israel and gives rules for cleanliness in the camp. Escaped slaves are not to be returned, and vows must be paid.
24|God gives laws about divorce, pledges and wages. Workers must be paid on the same day, and the poor, the stranger and the widow must be treated fairly.
25|God gives laws about fair punishment, marrying a dead brother's widow, honest weights and measures, and remembering Amalek.
26|Israel is to bring the first of the harvest to God and say a prayer remembering the Exodus. Every third year the tithe goes to the poor.
27|Israel is to set up stones with the law on them at Mount Ebal when they enter the land. The Levites pronounce curses on those who disobey.
28|Moses lists blessings for those who obey God and curses for those who do not. The curses are long and severe.
29|Moses calls Israel to renew the covenant on the plains of Moab. He warns that secret things belong to God, but what he has revealed belongs to the people.
30|Moses says that God will bring Israel back if they return to him. He sets before them life and death and says: choose life.
31|Moses tells Israel that he is 120 years old and Joshua will lead them. He writes down the law and gives it to the priests to read every seven years.
32|Moses teaches Israel a song that describes God as the Rock and warns what will happen if they turn away. God tells him to go up Mount Nebo and see the land.
33|Moses blesses each of the twelve tribes before he dies.
34|Moses climbs Mount Nebo and sees the promised land. He dies there at 120, and God buries him. Joshua takes his place, and no prophet since has been like Moses.
""")
B("Joshua", """
1|After Moses dies, God tells Joshua to be strong and courageous and to keep the law. Joshua prepares the people to cross the Jordan.
2|Joshua sends two spies to Jericho. Rahab hides them and, because she believes in God, she asks to be spared when the city is taken.
3|The priests carrying the ark step into the Jordan, and the water stops flowing. All Israel crosses on dry ground.
4|Israel sets up twelve stones from the riverbed as a memorial. Everyone has crossed the Jordan.
5|The men of Israel are circumcised at Gilgal, and they keep the Passover. The manna stops, and Joshua meets the commander of the Lord's army.
6|Israel marches around Jericho for seven days, and on the seventh day the walls fall down. Only Rahab and her family are saved.
7|Achan takes forbidden items from Jericho, and Israel loses a battle at Ai. Achan's sin is found out and he is punished.
8|With God's help Israel takes Ai. Joshua builds an altar on Mount Ebal and reads out the law, with blessings and curses.
9|The people of Gibeon trick Joshua into making a treaty by pretending to come from far away. When Israel finds out, they make the Gibeonites woodcutters and water carriers.
10|Five kings attack Gibeon. Joshua prays, and the sun stands still while Israel wins. Joshua conquers cities in the south.
11|Joshua defeats the northern kings at the waters of Merom. He has taken most of the land.
12|This chapter lists the thirty-one kings defeated by Moses and Joshua.
13|Joshua is old, and much land remains. God tells him to divide it among the tribes. The east side of the Jordan is given to Reuben, Gad and half of Manasseh.
14|Caleb, now eighty-five, asks for the hill country of Hebron and is given it, because he followed the Lord fully.
15|Judah receives its land, including Jerusalem's southern border. Caleb takes Hebron, and his daughter Achsah asks for springs of water.
16|The descendants of Joseph, Ephraim and Manasseh, receive their land in the center of Canaan.
17|Manasseh's land is described, including the daughters of Zelophehad's inheritance. The Joseph tribes ask for more land, and Joshua tells them to clear the forests.
18|The tent is set up at Shiloh. Joshua sends men to survey the rest of the land, and Benjamin's land is assigned.
19|The remaining tribes receive land: Simeon, Zebulun, Issachar, Asher, Naphtali and Dan. Joshua is given the town he asks for.
20|God tells Joshua to set up six cities of refuge, where a person who kills by accident can be safe.
21|The Levites receive forty-eight towns from the other tribes. The chapter ends by saying God gave Israel all he had promised.
22|The tribes east of the Jordan return home. They build an altar by the river, and the other tribes nearly go to war until they learn it is only a memorial.
23|Joshua, old, warns the leaders to keep God's law and not to mix with the nations left in the land.
24|At Shechem, Joshua recalls God's work and says: as for me and my household, we will serve the Lord. The people renew the covenant. Joshua dies at 110.
""")
B("Judges", """
1|After Joshua dies, Judah and Simeon fight the Canaanites, but several tribes fail to drive them out completely.
2|An angel tells Israel they have failed to obey God. After Joshua's generation dies, the people turn to idols, and God raises up judges to save them.
3|God leaves some nations in the land to test Israel. He raises up Othniel, then Ehud, who kills the Moabite king Eglon, and then Shamgar.
4|Deborah, a prophet and judge, calls Barak to fight Sisera, the Canaanite commander. Jael kills Sisera in her tent.
5|Deborah and Barak sing a song to celebrate God's victory over Sisera.
6|Midian oppresses Israel. The angel of the Lord calls Gideon, who tears down his father's altar to Baal and asks for signs with a fleece.
7|God tells Gideon to reduce his army to three hundred men. They surround the Midianite camp at night with trumpets and jars with torches and the enemy flees.
8|Gideon chases the Midianite kings and captures them. The people want him to be king, but he refuses. After his death Israel returns to Baal.
9|Gideon's son Abimelech kills his brothers and makes himself king of Shechem. After three years he is killed when a woman drops a millstone on him.
10|Tola and Jair judge Israel. The people again worship other gods, and God lets the Philistines and Ammonites oppress them. They cry to the Lord.
11|Jephthah, an outcast, leads Israel against Ammon. He makes a rash vow and, afterward, his daughter is the one who comes out to meet him.
12|Jephthah fights the Ephraimites who attack him. Three more judges follow: Ibzan, Elon and Abdon.
13|An angel appears to the wife of Manoah and tells her she will have a son, Samson, who will be a Nazirite and begin to save Israel from the Philistines.
14|Samson wants to marry a Philistine woman. He kills a lion, and later tells a riddle at his wedding. When the answer is revealed, he kills thirty men.
15|Samson burns the Philistines' fields with foxes and, with the jawbone of a donkey, kills a thousand men.
16|Samson loves Delilah. She finds out the secret of his strength and has his hair cut. The Philistines blind him, and in his last act he pulls down their temple.
17|Micah, from Ephraim, makes an idol and hires a Levite as his priest. The chapter ends by noting that there was no king in Israel and everyone did what was right in his own eyes.
18|The tribe of Dan takes Micah's idol and priest and conquers the city of Laish, which they rename Dan.
19|A Levite's concubine is abused to death by the men of Gibeah. The Levite cuts her body into twelve pieces and sends them to the tribes.
20|The tribes gather and demand that Benjamin hand over the guilty men. Benjamin refuses and Israel nearly wipes out the tribe in a civil war.
21|Israel regrets what it did to Benjamin and finds wives for the survivors. The book ends: in those days Israel had no king.
""")
B("Ruth", """
1|A famine drives Elimelech and Naomi from Bethlehem to Moab. Elimelech and their sons die. Naomi returns home, and her daughter-in-law Ruth refuses to leave her: your people will be my people, and your God my God.
2|Ruth gleans in the field of Boaz, a relative of Naomi's husband. Boaz is kind to her and tells his workers to leave extra grain for her.
3|Naomi tells Ruth to go to Boaz at the threshing floor at night. Boaz promises to act as their family redeemer if a closer relative does not.
4|Boaz settles the matter at the city gate, and he marries Ruth. Their son Obed is the grandfather of King David, and so an ancestor of Jesus.
""")
